A||||3||Black86SE||Best Way to Clean Engine Block||||1||
Z||000000||Black86SE||12-06-2001||07:23 PM||||What is the best way to clean all the crap off my block to get it ready for painting? I was thinking about pulling everything off/out and powerwashing it. Good idea or no?<BR>Need the info.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Black86SEsig_1.gif">  <BR>Black 1986 SE V6<BR>Automatic - 5-speed soon!<BR>17" Motegi MR7 Wheels<BR>P235/45/ZR17 <BR>BF Goodrich g-Force KDWS<BR>AIM ScreenName: Black86se||||reg||1
Z||000001||Rodrv6||12-06-2001||08:15 PM||||I've had good luck using liquid concentrate Wisk laundry liquid and a stiff brush and water.  It does a pretty good job of degreasing a bare engine block.  I like to remove all the external brackets and clean them all seperatly.  Make sure you wipe down the machined surfaces with a light coat of oil (WD-40 or such) so you don't get surface rust on the cylinder walls and such.  Most machine shops will "hot tank" the block, which will clean it paint and all!  The only downside is that the cam bearings will also be ruined and must be replaced.  (A good idea if you're rebuilding anyway)<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||12-06-2001||08:51 PM||||I just lay it on a metal dolly and roll it over to the car wash. stubborn places need a little help with putty knife or wire brush.<BR>wear a waterproof coat, hat and goggles. spray into all the little holes and passages.||||reg||1
Z||000003||tesmith66||12-07-2001||03:10 PM||||I power wash it and then use brake cleaner and a rag just before painting. (Let it dry completely first.)<P>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe||||reg||1
